re, the polynomial must have either:\n\n- All roots real. But given the nature of the polynomial with positive coefficients, except for the linear term, which is problematic because even for negative x, the function might be positive. Wait, actually, since the polynomial is of the form x^4 + ... +x^2 + ... +x +16, for x real, if x >=0, then all terms positive. If x is negative, however, x^4, x^2, and 16 are positive, while the coefficients of x^3, x^2, and x are negative. So for x <0, the terms like 14x^3 and 56x will be positive (since multiplied by negative x). Wait, maybe the polynomial is always positive for real x? Let's check with x = -1: f(-1)=1 -14 +52 -56 +16=1-14=-13 etc. As shown before, it can take negative values. So it does have real roots. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the roots are complex. If we can express the polynomial as a product of two quadratic polynomials with real coefficients, each having complex roots.\n\nAssume f(x) factors as (x^2 + px + q)(x^2 + rx + s), where pr is negative because the product of the quadratic coefficients (1)(1)=1, but the constant term is 16. Wait, but let's see:\n\nIf f(x)=(x^2 + px + q)(x^2 + rx + s), then:\n\np + r=14\n\npr + p q + r s=52\n\np s + q r=56\n\nq s=16\n\nWe need to solve real numbers p, q, r, s such that pr + pq + rs=52, p s + q r=56, q s=16.\n\nThis is similar to the previous integer case but with real numbers. It's possible that p, q, r, s are not integers. \n\nLet me attempt to solve these equations. From q s=16, let's set q =16/s. \n\nFrom first equation: r=14 -p. \n\nSubstitute into other equations:\n\npr + pq + rs= p(14 -p) + p*(16/s) + (14 -p)s=52\n\np(14 - p) +16p/s +s(14 -p)=52\n\nSecond equation: p s + q r= p s + (16/s)*r. Substitute r=14 -p:\n\np s + (16/s)(14 -p)=56\n\nSo,\n\np s + (16*14)/s -16p/s=56\n\nLet me multiply both equations by s to eliminate denominators:\n\nFirst equation multiplied by s:\n\np s^2 +16p + rs(14 -p)=52 s\n\nBut this is complicated.\n\nWait, let's focus on the second equation:\n\np s + (16/s)(14 -p) =56\n\nWhich can be written as:\n\np s + (224 -16p)/s =56\n\nMultiply both sides by s:\n\np s^2 +224 -16p=56 s\n\nThus,\n\np s^2 -16 p +224=56 s\n\nSimilarly, first equation:\n\np(14 -p) +16p/s +s(14 -p)=52\n\nThis seems more complex. Maybe we can relate the two equations. \n\nFrom second equation rearranged:\n\np(s^2 -16) =56 s -224\n\nThus,\n\np= (56 s -224)/(s^2 -16)\n\nAlso, from here:\n\np= (56 s -224)/(s^2 -16)= [56(s -4)]/(s^2 -16). Note that s^2 -16=(s-4)(s+4)\n\nSo p=56/(s +4)\n\nThus, p=56/(s+4)\n\nThen, let's substitute p=56/(s +4) into the first equation:\n\nOriginal first equation:\n\np(14 -p) +16p/s +s(14 -p)=52\n\nSubstitute p=56/(s+4):\n\n14 - p=14 -56/(s+4)= [14(s+4)-56]/(s+4)= [14s +56 -56]/(s+4)=14s/(s+4)\n\nThus, p(14 -p)= (56/(s +4)) * [14s/(s+4)]= (56 *14s)/(s+4)^2= (784 s)/(s +4)^2\n\nSimilarly, 16p/s=16*(56/(s+4))/s= (16*56)/(s(s +4))=896/(s(s+4))\n\nAnd s(14 -p)=s*14 -s*56/(s +4)=14s -56s/(s +4)= [14s(s +4) -56s]/(s +4)= [14s^2 +56s -56s]/(s +4)=14s^2/(s +4)\n\nThus, the first equation becomes:\n\n784 s/(s+4)^2 +896/(s(s +4)) +14s^2/(s +4)=52\n\nMultiply both sides by s(s +4)^2 to eliminate denominators:\n\n784 s * s +896(s +4) +14s^2 s=52 s(s +4)^2\n\nWait, let's check term by term:\n\nFirst term: 784 s * s =784 s2\n\nSecond term:896(s +4) =896s +3584\n\nThird term:14s2 s=14s3\n\nSo left-hand side (LHS):\n\n784 s2 +896s +3584 +14 s3\n\n=14 s3 +784 s2 +896 s +3584\n\nFourth term:52 s(s +4)^2=52 s(s2 +8s +16)=52 s3 +416 s2 +832 s\n\nThus, equation:\n\n14 s3 +784 s2 +896 s +3584 =52 s3 +416 s2 +832 s\n\nBring all terms to left-hand side:\n\n14s3 +784s2 +896s +3584 -52s3 -416s2 -832s =0\n\nCalculate:\n\n(14s3 -52s3) + (784s2 -416s2) + (896s -832s) +3584= (-38 s3) + 368 s2 +64 s +3584=0\n\nSo:\n\n-38 s3 +368 s2 +64 s +3584=0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n38 s3 -368 s2 -64 s -3584=0\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n19 s3 -184 s2 -32 s -1792=0\n\nNow, need to solve for s. Let's try rational roots. Possible rational roots with integer coefficients are factors of 1792 divided by 19. Possible roots: ±1, ±2, ±4, ±7, ±8, ±14, ±16, etc., scaled by 19. Let's test s=14:\n\n19*(14)^3 -184*(14)^2 -32*14 -1792=19*2744 -184*196 -448 -1792\n\nCalculate:\n\n19*2744=52136\n\n184*196=35824\n\nSo 52136 -35824=16312\n\n16312 -448=15864\n\n15864 -1792=14072≠0. Not root.\n\ns=16:\n\n19*4096 -184*256 -32*16 -1792=19*4096=77824; 184*256=46 624; 32*16=512\n\n77824 -46624=31200; 31200 -512=30688; 30688 -1792=28896≠0.\n\ns=7:\n\n19*343 -184*49 -32*7 -1792=6517 -9024 -224 -1792\n\n=6517 -9024= -2507; -2507 -224= -2731; -2731 -1792= -4523≠0.\n\ns= 28:\n\n19*(28)^3 -184*(28)^2 -32*28 -1792\n\nCalculate:\n\n28^2=784; 28^3=21952\n\n19*21952=417088\n\n184*784=143360\n\nSo 417088 -143360=273728\n\n273728 -32*28=273728 -896=272832 -1792=271040≠0.\n\ns= 12:\n\n19*1728 -184*144 -32*12 -1792=19*1728=32832; 184*144=26544; 32*12=384\n\n32832 -26544=6288; 6288 -384=5904; 5904 -1792=4112≠0.\n\nAnother approach: use the equation 19 s3 -184 s2 -32 s -1792=0. Let's attempt to factor it. Divide by leading term s3:\n\n19 s3 -184 s2 -32 s -1792=0\n\nIf it factors as (s - a)(19 s2 + bs +c)=0, expanding gives19 s3 + (b -19a) s2 + (c -19b) s + (-19c)=0. Comparing with original equation, constants:\n\n-19c= -1792 → c=1792/19≈94.368... Not integer. Thus, unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, try using the rational root theorem again. Given the coefficients, possible rational roots are factors of 1792 divided by 19. Let me check s= -7:\n\n19*(-7)^3 -184*(-7)^2 -32*(-7) -1792=19*(-343) -184*(49) +224 -1792\n\n= -6517 -9024 +224 -1792= (-6517 -9024) + (-1792) +224= (-15541) -1792 +224= -17529 +224= -17305≠0.\n\nNo luck. This suggests that there are no rational roots, so s is irrational. Therefore, solving this cubic equation would be complex. Maybe we need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, given that the quartic polynomial has real coefficients, all the coefficients real, so complex roots come in pairs. Suppose the roots are z1, z2 and their conjugates \\overline{z1}, \\overline{z2}. Then using Vieta's formulas:\n\nz1 + z2 + \\overline{z1} + \\overline{z2} = -14. This is equal to 2Re(z1) +2Re(z2)= -14⇒Re(z1) +Re(z2)= -7.\n\nSimilarly, the product z1z2\\overline{z1}\\overline{z2}= |z1|2 |z2|2=16. So |z1|2 |z2|2=16⇒|z1||z2|=4 or |z1|=|z2|=2.\n\nAlso, from the symmetric sums, such as z1\\overline{z1} + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1} + z2\\overline{z2}=52. But z\\overline{z}= |z|2. So this sum is |z1|2 + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1} + |z2|2=52. So |z1|2 + |z2|2 + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1}=52. Note that z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1}= z1\\overline{z2} + \\overline{z1}z2 (by taking conjugate of z1\\overline{z2}) which is 2 Re(z1\\overline{z2}). Therefore,\n\n|z1|2 + |z2|2 + 2 Re(z1\\overline{z2})=52.\n\nWe also know that z1 + z2 are complex numbers, but since we are dealing with real parts and magnitudes, this could get complicated.\n\nLet me denote z1=a+bi, z2=c+di where a,b,c,d real. Then \\overline{z1}=a -bi, \\overline{z2}=c -di.\n\nThus, Re(z1)=a, Im(z1)=b, Re(z2)=c, Im(z2)=d.\n\nFrom the sum:\n\nz1 + z2 + \\overline{z1} + \\overline{z2} = (a+bi +c+di) + (a -bi +c -di)= 2a +2c= -14⇒a +c= -7.\n\nThe product |z1||z2|=4 (since |z1|2 |z2|2=16 ⇒ |z1||z2|=4).\n\nAlso, from the other sums:\n\n|z1|2 + |z2|2 + 2 Re(z1\\overline{z2})=52.\n\nExpressed in terms of a,b,c,d:\n\n(a2 +b2) + (c2 +d2) + 2[(ac + bd)] =52.\n\nWe also have a +c= -7 and bc +ad= something... Wait, let's recall Vieta's for the quartic:\n\nSum of products of roots taken two at a time: z1z2 + z1z3 + z1z4 + z2z3 + z2z4 + z3z4=52.\n\nIn our case, since the roots are z1,z2,\\overline{z1},\\overline{z2}, let's compute the sum:\n\nz1z2 + z1\\overline{z1} + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1} + z2\\overline{z2} + \\overline{z1}\\overline{z2}\n\n= z1z2 + |z1|2 + |z2|2 + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1}\n\nBut z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1}= 2 Re(z1\\overline{z2})\n\nSo this sum is |z1|2 + |z2|2 + z1\\overline{z2} + ... Hmm, confused.\n\nAlternatively, going back:\n\nIn the quartic's case, the sum of all products two at a time is 52. So that includes:\n\n- The four roots taken two at a time, two complex and two real: z1,z2; z1,z3; z1,z4; z2,z3; etc.\n\nBut since z3=\\overline{z1}, z4=\\overline{z2}, the products:\n\nz1\\overline{z1}, z2\\overline{z2}, z1\\overline{z2}, z2\\overline{z1}, z1 z2, etc.\n\nBut in total, sum of all pairwise products:\n\n= 2(z1\\overline{z1} + z2\\overline{z2}) + z1\\overline{z2} + z2\\overline{z1} + z1 z2 + others.\n\nWait, maybe not. Wait, we have four roots: z1, z2, z3=z1 conjugate, z4=z2 conjugate. So sum of all pairs:\n\nz1z2, z1z3, z1z4, z1z5, z2z3, z2z4, z3z4.\n\nBut z3= \\overline{z1}, z4= \\overline{z2}\n\nSo:\n\nz1z2 + z1 \\overline{z1} + z1 \\overline{z2} + z2 \\overline{z1} + z2 \\overline{z2} + \\overline{z1}\\overline{z2} + \\overline{z1} \\overline{z1} + \\overline{z1}\\overline{z2} + \\overline{z2}\\overline{z2}\n\nWait, this is getting too messy. Original quartic: coefficients in terms of the roots.\n\nBut since the quartic is f(x) =x^4 +14x3+52x2+56x+16, the sum of the products two at a time is 52. So computed via coefficient 52, which is known from Vieta.\n\nAlternatively, since z1 and z2 are complex, and their conjugates are z3,z4. \n\nGiven that, perhaps use the fact that sum of roots = -14.\n\nIf the roots can be grouped as complex conjugate pairs, then the sum of each pair would be real.\n\nSuppose that the roots are z1= a+bi, z2= a -bi, z3= c+di, z4= c -di. Then sum z1 + z2 + z3 + z4= 2a + 2c= -14 ⇒ a +c= -7.\n\nSum of products of roots (pairwise):\n\nz1z2= (a+bi)(a -bi)=a2 +b2\n\nz1z3= (a+bi)(c+di)=ac + adi + bci - bd\n\nz1z4= (a+bi)(c -di)=ac - adi + bci + bd\n\nz2z3= (a -bi)(c+di)=ac +adi -bci -bd\n\nz2z4= (a -bi)(c -di)=ac -adi -bci -bd\n\nz3z4= (c+di)(c -di)=c2 +d2\n\nAlso, other pairs:\n\nz1z2 + z1z3 + z1z4 + z2z3 + z2z4 + z3z4\n\n= (a2 +b2) + [z1z3 + z1z4 + z2z3 + z2z4]\n\nNote that z1z3 + z1z4 + z2z3 + z2z4= (a+bi)